WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.610
We are now ready to discuss
the most difficult challenge in

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:06.060
applications of Hidden Markov Models:

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.450
how to find out the parameters of the HMM.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.510
So far,
we have assumed that the transition and

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:16.520
emission probabilities
of the HMM are known.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.707
For example, when you walked
into the crooked casino,

00:00:19.707 --> 00:00:25.740
you knew that the dealer changes
the coins with probability 0.1,

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.040
and you also knew the biases
of both the fair and biased coins.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:36.905
However, imagine that you only know that
the crooked dealer is using two coins,

00:00:36.905 --> 00:00:39.440
and know nothing else,

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.900
know nothing about probabilities
of the biased and

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.250
fair coins, or probabilities
of changing between coins.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.070
Suppose you observe the sequence.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:55.150
How can you figure out what
are the biases of the coins, and

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:59.230
how often the dealer changes the coin?

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:04.360
Can we develop an algorithm for
parameter estimation for an arbitrary HMM?

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:10.500
Let's start answering this difficult
question from the unlikely scenario,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.350
when the dealer reveals
what is the hidden path.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:19.540
In this case, we will face the HMM
parameter estimation problem:

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:25.620
Find optimal parameters explaining
the emitted string and the hidden path.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.150
So the hidden path and emitted string
are given at the bottom of the slide.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.240
How would we derive transition and
emission probabilities?

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:39.510
In the case that the hidden path is known,
let's define T_(l,k)

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:44.710
as th number of transitions from state
l to state k in the hidden path pi.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:52.410
For example, in this case, the number of
transitions from b to f is equal to five.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:57.230
And some common sense suggests to us,
since the transition probabilities from

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:03.080
state l to state k can be defined as
the number of transitions from state

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:08.420
l to state k, divided by the number
of all transitions from state l.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.430
And in this case it
will be five over nine.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.820
Now, after we

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.740
estimated the probability of
transition from state l to state k,

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:27.570
let's now estimate the emission probability
of symbol b from state k.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:31.990
And to do this,
let's define the parameter E_k(b),

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:38.210
the number of times symbol b is emitted
when path pi is in state k.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:44.440
In our case, E_F(T) will be equal to 6,

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:50.120
and common sense again suggests that
you define emission probability of symbol b

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:55.630
from state k as the number of times
symbol b is emitted in state k,

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:59.900
divided by the total number of
symbols emitted in state k.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:00.650
In our case, that's 6/11.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:05.770
It turns out that transmission and

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.360
emission probabilities
defined in this way

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:14.970
define the optimal solution of
the HMM parameter estimation problem.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:19.990
However, the real problem we
are trying to solve is the problem when

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:24.685
the dealer is not that generous,
so neither HiddenPath nor Parameters

00:03:24.685 --> 00:03:30.605
are known, and it results in the
HMM Parameter Learning Problem,

00:03:30.605 --> 00:03:34.105
a more difficult problem than the
HMM Parameter Estimation Problem.

00:03:34.105 --> 00:03:39.555
In the HMM Parameter Learning Problem, we
need to estimate the parameters of an HMM

00:03:39.555 --> 00:03:43.429
explaining an emitted string.
Only the emitted string is known.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:50.380
So, let's try the following algorithm
for solving this problem.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.590
We are only given the emitted string, but

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:56.840
let's start from an arbitrary
wild guess of parameters.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:00.220
As soon as we guess the parameters,

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:05.000
we can derive the hidden path by
solving the Decoding Problem.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.150
As soon as hidden path is known, we can
pretend that we don't know the parameters,

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:15.347
and we can solve the HMM Parameter Estimation
Problem, deriving a new set of parameters.

00:04:15.347 --> 00:04:22.210
Afterwards, we can once again forget about
the hidden path we have constructed,

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:28.300
and use the emitted string and the new set of
parameters to derive the new hidden path.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.240
And iterate.

00:04:30.315 --> 00:04:36.205
This algorithm is called
Viterbi Learning for HMM.

00:04:36.205 --> 00:04:41.545
The deficiency of Viterbi Learning is
that it is based on the Viterbi algorithm

00:04:41.545 --> 00:04:46.785
that gives a strict yes or
no answer to the question "Was

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:52.160
the HMM in state k at time i,
given that it emitted string x?"

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:59.770
This question fails to account for how
certain we are in the yes or no answer.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:04.900
And in the next section, we'll try to
design a soft answer to this question

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.050
and design a new parameter
of learning algorithm

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:10.320
that improves on Viterbi Learning.

